  "liner": {
   "body_md": "## What listeners single out\n\n- **One In Seven** (2 reviews): Neal Evans opened it with a techno-style remix intro, making it the best version the reviewer has heard; The intro was insane, with Neil playing synth [3][4]\n\n## The recordings\n\n- **soulive2001-12-15.shnf** (recommended): source not stated; 9 songs; 116 min; rated 4.67/5 by 3 [1]. Said about its sound: Reviewer says the sound Rak helped recreate does the best justice to the experience of the show [5]\n- **Soulive2001-12-15sbd.shn**: matrix (soundboard mixed with audience mics); 8 songs; 115 min [2]. Said about its sound: The first seven minutes of the soundboard were distorted, so they were patched in from another source [2]\n\n## Around the show\n\n- The reviewer was new to Soulive, taped the show, and says bands tend to play their best at the Temple Bar [3]\n- Venue's posted capacity is under 200, yet over 300 people packed in; this was the last of five Soulive shows there [4]\n- The first set is the reviewer's favorite Soulive set ever and the best of the five Temple Bar shows [4]\n\n## Tapers and lineage\n\n- **soulive2001-12-15.shnf**: taped by Andrew Rakow; source: (onstage) Neumann KM 140 (x-y front of neal, chest high) > Lunatec V2 > Apogee AD-1000 > DAT [1]. Also: The reviewer taped the show personally and notes Soulive is a difficult band to record [3]\n- **Soulive2001-12-15sbd.shn**: taped by Michel Pacifici; transferred by Michel Pacifici; source: SBD/onstage Neumann KM140 (ortf @ edge of b3, 4'high) > Snake > V2 + MonoSBD> Mackie1202VLZ> Apogee AD500e @ 16/44.1> Sony D8 [2]. Also: The opening of steppin' was filled with Rak's onstage KM140 recording, cross-dissolved at seven minutes in Pro Tools 24mix+ [2]",
